@** The scene. Everything the viewer knows about a USD file passes through
one object of class [[Scene]]. It has three jobs. First, it opens the stage and
mirrors its prims into an EnTT \emph{registry}, so that the user interface can
walk the hierarchy and attach information to any prim. Second, it resolves the
materials of the scene: it follows UsdShade connections to the inputs of the
UsdPreviewSurface shader, and loads the textures they name. Third, it
\emph{flattens} every visible piece of geometry into a few large arrays of
world-space triangles, which is the only form of geometry the renderer
understands. Along the way it also collects the cameras of the stage, so that
the viewer can look through them.

The declarations live in \lpfile{Scene.h} and the code in \lpfile{Scene.cpp}.
The header is short enough to be read as a table of contents for this part.

<<UsdOptixViewer/src/Scene.h>>=
<<Headers needed by the scene declarations>>

<<The PrimInfo component>>

<<The Hierarchy component>>

<<The MeshGeometry component>>

<<The CameraView component>>

<<Texture wrap modes and decoded textures>>

<<The ScalarInput material parameter>>

<<The Material record>>

<<The flattened world-space geometry>>

<<A camera of the stage>>

<<The Scene class>>
@ The header needs the standard containers, EnTT, and two USD headers: the
stage itself and the UsdShade material class, which appears in the
signature of a private member function.

<<Headers needed by the scene declarations>>=
// A USD stage mirrored into an EnTT registry: one entity per prim, with
// components describing its place in the hierarchy and its geometry.
#pragma once

#include <array>
#include <map>
#include <string>
#include <tuple>
#include <vector>

#include <entt/entt.hpp>
#include <pxr/usd/usd/stage.h>
#include <pxr/usd/usdShade/material.h>
@* Entities and components. EnTT is an \emph{entity--component--system}
library. An entity is nothing but an integer handle; data are attached to it as
\emph{components}, plain structures stored in dense arrays, one array per
component type. We create one entity for every prim of the stage, including the
invisible pseudo-root ``[[/]]''. This costs little and gives every prim an
identity that the user interface can store (the current selection is an
entity) and that other parts of the program can decorate: only the prims that
produce triangles receive a [[MeshGeometry]] component, so ``is this prim
drawn?'' is simply ``does this entity have that component?''.

Every entity carries a [[PrimInfo]], which names the prim it stands for. The
path is the key back into the USD stage; the name and the type name are copied
because the hierarchy panel shows them for every visible row, every frame.

<<The PrimInfo component>>=
// ---- components -----------------------------------------------------------
struct PrimInfo
{
    pxr::SdfPath path;
    std::string  name;
    std::string  typeName;
};
@ The tree structure is stored explicitly. USD can of course enumerate the
children of a prim, but doing so through the stage on every frame of the user
interface would be wasteful, and the list would have to be filtered the same
way every time. A [[Hierarchy]] keeps the parent and the children in USD's
order. The root's parent is [[entt::null]].

<<The Hierarchy component>>=
struct Hierarchy
{
    entt::entity parent = entt::null;
    std::vector<entt::entity> children;
};
@ A prim that contributed triangles remembers where they went. Its triangles
occupy a contiguous range of the flattened arrays, from [[firstTriangle]] on;
[[meshId]] is the small integer that the renderer writes into its picking
buffer, so that a click on the image can be mapped back to the entity. The
remaining fields are for the inspector: the index of the material bound to the
prim, how many GeomSubsets override it, and whether texture coordinates were
found.

<<The MeshGeometry component>>=
// Present on prims that contributed triangles to the rendered geometry.
struct MeshGeometry
{
    unsigned int meshId;
    unsigned int firstTriangle;
    unsigned int triangleCount;
    unsigned int material;        // material bound to the prim itself
    unsigned int subsetMaterials;  // number of GeomSubsets with their own material
    bool hasUVs;
};
@ Camera prims receive a [[CameraView]] component instead. It only holds the
index of the camera in the scene's list of cameras (described with the
cameras, at the end of this part), so that the inspector can offer to look
through the selected prim.

<<The CameraView component>>=
// Present on UsdGeomCamera prims: index into Scene::cameras().
struct CameraView
{
    unsigned int index;
};
@ The [[Scene]] class is small from the outside. A scene is either empty or
holds one open stage; [[load]] replaces whatever was there. The accessors give
the user interface the stage, the registry, the root entity, the flattened
geometry, the cameras and the list of warnings collected while reading the file. The up
axis matters to the camera, which must know whether ``up'' is $+y$ or $+z$.

<<The Scene class>>=
