@* Opening a stage. Loading starts from a clean slate. [[UsdStage::Open]]
composes the file with everything it references; if that fails, the error
message is returned to the caller, who shows it in a dialog.

Two global properties of the stage are recorded. The up axis is part of the
stage metadata and defaults to $y$. The time is the start of the authored
time range if there is one, and USD's \emph{default} time otherwise: the
viewer shows a single moment, and for an animated file the first frame is the
natural choice. Then we build the entities, flatten the geometry and collect
the cameras.

<<Open a stage and build the scene>>=
bool Scene::load(const std::string& path, std::string& error)
{
    clear();
    UsdStageRefPtr stage = UsdStage::Open(path);
    if (!stage)
    {
        error = "Could not open USD stage: " + path;
        return false;
    }
    m_stage = stage;
    m_filePath = path;
    m_zUp = UsdGeomGetStageUpAxis(stage) == UsdGeomTokens->z;
    m_time = stage->HasAuthoredTimeCodeRange() ? UsdTimeCode(stage->GetStartTimeCode()) : UsdTimeCode::Default();
    m_root = createEntities(stage->GetPseudoRoot(), entt::null);
    extractGeometry();
    extractCameras(m_root);
    return true;
}
@ Clearing destroys every entity, forgets the cameras and empties both caches. Releasing the
stage pointer closes the stage, unless somebody else still holds it.

<<Forget the current stage>>=
void Scene::clear()
{
    m_registry.clear();
    m_root = entt::null;
    m_geometry = FlatGeometry{};
    m_cameras.clear();
    m_warnings.clear();
    m_materialCache.clear();
    m_textureCache.clear();
    m_stage = nullptr;
    m_filePath.clear();
}
@ Problems found while reading a file are not errors: an unsupported shader
or a missing texture should not prevent us from showing the rest of the scene.
They are collected as warnings, which the inspector lists. A large scene can
produce the same complaint for hundreds of prims, so each distinct message is
kept only once.

<<Collect a warning once>>=
void Scene::warn(const std::string& message)
{
    if (std::find(m_warnings.begin(), m_warnings.end(), message) == m_warnings.end())
        m_warnings.push_back(message);
}
@ Going from an entity back to its prim is a path lookup on the stage. An
invalid entity (for instance [[entt::null]], meaning ``nothing selected'')
yields an invalid prim, which callers test with [[if (prim)]].

<<Find the prim of an entity>>=
UsdPrim Scene::prim(entt::entity e) const
{
    if (!m_stage || !m_registry.valid(e))
        return {};
    return m_stage->GetPrimAtPath(m_registry.get<PrimInfo>(e).path);
}
@ The hierarchy is copied by a recursive walk. Each entity gets its
[[PrimInfo]] before its children are visited, so the entities are created in
depth-first preorder, and its [[Hierarchy]] after, when the children's handles
are known.

The predicate [[UsdTraverseInstanceProxies]] deserves a word. USD shares the
contents of \emph{instanceable} prims: all instances of a model point to a
single hidden \emph{prototype}, and by default the children of an instance are
not visible at all. With this predicate, the walk descends into every instance
and yields \emph{instance proxies}, read-only stand-ins for the prototype's
prims at the instance's location. Each instance therefore gets its own
entities, its own row in the tree and, later, its own copy of the triangles.
That is wasteful for scenes with thousands of instances, but it keeps the
renderer simple: there is just one bag of triangles.

<<Mirror the prim hierarchy into the registry>>=
entt::entity Scene::createEntities(const UsdPrim& prim, entt::entity parent)
{
    const entt::entity e = m_registry.create();
    m_registry.emplace<PrimInfo>(e, prim.GetPath(), prim.IsPseudoRoot() ? std::string("/") : prim.GetName().GetString(),
                                 prim.GetTypeName().GetString());
    Hierarchy hierarchy{parent, {}};
    for (const UsdPrim& child : prim.GetFilteredChildren(UsdTraverseInstanceProxies()))
        hierarchy.children.push_back(createEntities(child, e));
    m_registry.emplace<Hierarchy>(e, std::move(hierarchy));
    return e;
}
@* The flattened geometry. The renderer builds a single acceleration
structure over all the triangles of the scene, already placed in world space.
[[FlatGeometry]] is that soup, together with everything a hit point needs in
order to be shaded. Positions are shared between the triangles of one prim and
addressed by [[indices]]; all the other arrays are \emph{per triangle}, so that
the GPU, which learns only the index of the triangle that was hit, can find
its mesh, its material, the texture coordinates of its three corners and the
shading normals of its three corners. A zero normal means ``no shading normal;
use the flat normal of the triangle''. Materials and textures are kept in
arrays and referred to by index. Finally, [[meshEntity]] maps a [[meshId]] back
to its entity, and the bounds let the camera frame the whole scene.

The cameras of the stage are kept next to the geometry, in a list of
[[SceneCamera]] records, one per [[UsdGeomCamera]] prim. Like the geometry,
each is evaluated once, at the scene's time, and stored in world space. A USD
camera is described by its transform and by physical lens parameters: a focal
length and the size of the film back (the \emph{aperture}), in tenths of a
scene unit for orthographic cameras. What the renderer wants is simpler: an
eye point, a forward and an up direction, and the half size of the
\emph{window}, the part of the image plane that the camera sees. For a
perspective camera the window lies at distance 1, so its half height is the
tangent of half the vertical field of view; for an orthographic camera it is
measured in scene units. The viewport rarely has the shape of the camera's
film back, so the window is fitted to it later, in the application. The
vertical field of view is kept for the inspector.

<<The flattened world-space geometry>>=
// Geometry flattened to world space, ready to upload to the renderer.
struct FlatGeometry
{
    std::vector<float>        vertices;     // xyz
    std::vector<unsigned int> indices;      // 3 per triangle
    std::vector<unsigned int> triMesh;      // mesh id per triangle
    std::vector<unsigned int> triMaterial;  // material per triangle
    std::vector<float>        triUVs;       // 6 per triangle (st of each corner)
    std::vector<float>        triNormals;   // 9 per triangle (shading normal of each corner, 0 = flat)
    std::vector<Material>     materials;
    std::vector<Texture>      textures;
    std::vector<entt::entity> meshEntity;   // entity of each mesh id
    float boundsMin[3] = {0, 0, 0};
    float boundsMax[3] = {0, 0, 0};

    bool empty() const { return indices.empty(); }
};
@
<<A camera of the stage>>=
// A UsdGeomCamera at the stage's start time, in world space. The window is
// the image plane at distance 1 (perspective) or the view size in scene
// units (orthographic); the viewer fits it to the viewport's aspect ratio.
struct SceneCamera
{
    entt::entity entity = entt::null;
    std::string path;
    std::array<float, 3> eye = {0, 0, 0}, forward = {0, 0, -1}, up = {0, 1, 0};
    float halfWidth = 1.0f, halfHeight = 1.0f;
    bool orthographic = false;
    float clipNear = 0.0f, clipFar = 1e16f;
    float fovYDeg = 0.0f;  // vertical field of view of a perspective camera
};
@ Flattening is one pass over the entities. For each prim that should be
drawn we produce its triangles in local space, decide the material of each
face, read its texture coordinates, transform it to world space and append it
to the arrays. Each of these steps gets a section of its own.

<<Flatten the stage into world-space triangles>>=
// ---- geometry ---------------------------------------------------------------
void Scene::extractGeometry()
{
    <<Prepare the flattening of the stage>>

    for (auto [e, info] : m_registry.view<PrimInfo>().each())
    {
        <<Skip prims that are not visible renderable geometry>>

        <<Triangulate a prim in its local space>>

        <<Assign a material to every face of a prim>>

        <<Read the texture coordinates of a prim>>

        <<Transform the points of a prim to world space>>

        <<Append the triangles of a prim to the flat geometry>>

        <<Warn about textured prims without texture coordinates>>
    }

    <<Record the bounds of the scene>>
}
@ A [[UsdGeomXformCache]] computes local-to-world matrices and remembers the
matrices of ancestors, so that siblings deep in the tree do not recompute the
same chain of transforms. The bounds start empty, at $+\infty$ and $-\infty$.

<<Prepare the flattening of the stage>>=
UsdGeomXformCache xformCache(m_time);
FlatGeometry& g = m_geometry;
float lo[3] = {INFINITY, INFINITY, INFINITY}, hi[3] = {-INFINITY, -INFINITY, -INFINITY};
@ Only \emph{gprims}, the geometric primitives of UsdGeom, can be drawn.
Visibility is inherited, so we ask USD to \emph{compute} it rather than read
the prim's own attribute: a mesh under an invisible transform is invisible.
The \emph{purpose} works the same way; we draw what a final render would draw
(purpose [[default]] or [[render]]) and skip the [[proxy]] and [[guide]]
geometry that applications use for previews and manipulators.

<<Skip prims that are not visible renderable geometry>>=
const UsdPrim p = m_stage->GetPrimAtPath(info.path);
if (!p || !p.IsA<UsdGeomGprim>())
    continue;
const UsdGeomImageable imageable(p);
const TfToken purpose = imageable.ComputePurpose();
if (imageable.ComputeVisibility(m_time) == UsdGeomTokens->invisible ||
    (purpose != UsdGeomTokens->default_ && purpose != UsdGeomTokens->render))
    continue;
@* From prims to triangles. Three kinds of gprims are supported: polygonal
meshes, and the implicit cube and sphere. Each is converted into a
[[LocalMesh]], a triangle soup in the prim's own coordinate system. The other
gprims (cylinders, cones, capsules, curves, points) produce no triangles and
are skipped. The default sizes are USD's: a cube of edge~2 and a sphere of
radius~1.

<<Triangulate a prim in its local space>>=
LocalMesh local;
if (p.IsA<UsdGeomMesh>())
    meshFromUsdMesh(UsdGeomMesh(p), m_time, local);
else if (p.IsA<UsdGeomCube>())
{
    double size = 2.0;
    UsdGeomCube(p).GetSizeAttr().Get(&size, m_time);
    meshFromCube(size, local);
}
else if (p.IsA<UsdGeomSphere>())
{
    double radius = 1.0;
    UsdGeomSphere(p).GetRadiusAttr().Get(&radius, m_time);
    meshFromSphere(radius, local);
}
if (local.indices.empty())
    continue;
@ A triangle soup must remember more than three point indices per triangle.
USD attaches data to meshes at different rates, called \emph{interpolations}:
once per point ([[vertex]] or [[varying]]), once per face ([[uniform]]), once
per corner of each face ([[faceVarying]]), or once for the whole mesh
([[constant]]). To fetch the right value for each corner of each triangle
later, we keep, besides the point index, the \emph{face-vertex} index of the
corner (its position in USD's [[faceVertexIndices]] array) and the index of
the face the triangle came from. The face index also serves GeomSubsets, which
assign materials to sets of faces. The shading normals, when there are any,
are resolved per corner right away.

<<Local triangle soup of one prim>>=
// Local-space triangle soup produced for one prim.
struct LocalMesh
{
    std::vector<GfVec3f> points;
    std::vector<unsigned int> indices;     // 3 point indices per triangle
    std::vector<unsigned int> cornerFv;    // 3 face-vertex indices per triangle
    std::vector<unsigned int> faceOfTri;   // source face of each triangle
    std::vector<GfVec3f> cornerNormals;    // 3 shading normals per triangle, empty = flat
    size_t faceCount = 1;
};
@ A [[UsdGeomMesh]] describes polygons by three arrays: the points, the
number of vertices of each face, and the concatenated vertex indices of all
faces. Converting it takes four steps.

<<Convert a UsdGeomMesh into a local triangle soup>>=
bool meshFromUsdMesh(const UsdGeomMesh& mesh, UsdTimeCode time, LocalMesh& out)
{
